The background is black.\n   192\t    - Several colored fragments are scattered on the grid; each fragment is a single-color piece and different fragments use different colors.\n   193\t    - Fragments come in complementary pairs: inside equal-sized bounding boxes, one fragment occupies exactly the cells the other fragment leaves empty.\n   194\t    - A small 1x2 legend sits near the top-right corner inside a grey border; its left color means \"larger fragment\" and its right color means \"smaller fragment\".\n   195\t\n   196\t    Rule:\n   197\t    - Find fragment pairs by matching equal bounding-box sizes and complementary occupancy masks.\n   198\t    - For each pair, keep the rectangle at the piece whose bounding-box top row is smaller, and translate the other piece so the two bounding boxes coincide there.\n   199\t    - Recolor the merged rectangle: the larger-area fragment takes the legend's left color and the smaller-area fragment takes the legend's right color.\n   200\t    - If the two fragment areas are equal, keep their original colors instead of using the legend colors; erase the moved-away original position to black and keep the legend unchanged.\n   201\t\n   202\t    Key insight:\n   203\t    - The hard part is not matching identical shapes but noticing that the two masks are exact complements inside the same window, then using the separate legend to recolor by area ranking rather than by source color.\n   204\t    \"\"\"\n   205\t    return _solve(grid)\n   206\t\n   207\t\n   208\tdef _can_place(rect: tuple[int, int, int, int], others: Iterable[tuple[int, int, int, int]], pad: int = 1) -> bool:\n   209\t    r0, c0, h, w = rect\n   210\t    for or0, oc0, oh, ow in others:\n   211\t        separated = (\n   212\t            r0 + h + pad <= or0\n   213\t            or or0 + oh + pad <= r0\n   214\t            or c0 + w + pad <= oc0\n   215\t            or oc0 + ow + pad <= c0\n   216\t        )\n   217\t        if not separated:\n   218\t            